Z||000000||zeusrekning||08-23-2000||08:56 PM||||I tore apart my '74 honda 350 - four cylinder.<BR>I dont want to tear it apart to much. the cylinder walls looks good but there is alot of carbon build up on top of the pistons. Is there any way to remove the carbon build up with out tearing apart the bottom end of the block? this engine will go onto a lawn mower?<BR>what happens if i don't remove the carbon build up. what else should be done. like lapping the valves?||||reg||5
Z||000001||gixxer||08-24-2000||10:20 PM||||If the engine is high miles, I'd bite the bullet and replace the cam chain. Of course that would mean that the cases would come apart. Get a shop manual and that shouldn't me a big deal. <BR>I'd like to say that I've had some experience with rebuilding this engine, as I've had 2 400 fours, but they never gave me any problems, so I never opened them up.<BR>||||reg||1
